{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3535","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3535","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3535\",\"parent_revision\":\"94a7f556dc199820ddfff08e55c22b5c3986a6c30a2673942bbe9487e9fd77b3\",\"root_revision\":\"94a7f556dc199820ddfff08e55c22b5c3986a6c30a2673942bbe9487e9fd77b3\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-13\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`.claude/agents/research-insight-extractor.md` files backlog items from its proposals. Filing is the irreversible step in that agent's run: an item outlives the proposal file, gets groomed, and gets worked. The review that audits these claims (`@research-curator --review`, entry-review-rubric Gate 4) runs *after* the agent returns, so nothing downstream catches a bad claim before it is already tracked.\\n\\nIn-flight work on branch `research-curator/relevance-anchors` (PR #3530) adds the verification half of this: an `**Absence evidence**` field requiring the exact search and its result behind any absence claim, and the rule that every path a proposal names is opened before the proposal is written, with a Target-state path confirmed absent rather than confirmed present. What it does not add is a consequence \u2014 the agent verifies, but a failed verification does not stop the item being filed.\\n\\nThe gate must do all of the following:\\n\\n1. Run at the filing step specifically, before any backlog item is created, not only at proposal-writing time. Filing is the irreversible one.\\n2. Disqualify a proposal from filing when either check fails, and record the reason verbatim in the improvements file rather than dropping it silently \u2014 `Backlog: Deferred \u2014 cited path does not exist: {path}` for a failed path check, `Backlog: Deferred \u2014 absence not searched for` for an absence claim with no recorded search, however confident the reasoning around it reads.\\n3. Never repair a near-miss path on the writer's behalf, and never file the item with the bad path stripped out \u2014 the proposal rested on that path, so removing it changes what was proposed rather than fixing it.\\n4. Record evidence that **re-runs or re-locates**, in the proposal's `**Verified**` field. For a presence claim: the repository path plus the exact quoted text the claim rests on. For an absence claim: the search command and its result. Both forms are recoverable by a later reader with no more than the file in front of them.\\n5. Exempt a `### Target state` creation path from the existence check \u2014 its absence is the reason the proposal exists. Verify instead that the parent directory it would go into exists.\\n\\n**Evidence must not depend on a line number.** A `file:line` reference stored in a durable artifact is stale by design: it is a coordinate into a mutable file, correct in something read immediately and wrong in a backlog item or an analysis file that lives for months, because any edit above that line silently invalidates it. The quoted text is the durable locator \u2014 content-addressed and recoverable by grep. The line number is the only part that rots, and it adds nothing grep would not find. A line number may appear as a convenience, but nothing may depend on it and no check may treat it as authoritative.\\n\\nThis repo has already proved the weaker form of that failure. PR #3391's rename and PR #3422's removals broke roughly 99 citations across 83 entries, all correct when written \u2014 and those were paths. Line numbers are more fragile than paths, not less.\\n\\nPR #3530 got this right for absence evidence and left it inconsistent for presence evidence. Its absence case records `grep -ril \\\"{term}\\\" {scope} -> 0 matches`, a command any reader can re-run. Its presence case is uneven: the `entry-template.md` anchor record and the Phase 1c `REPO ANCHORS` format do quote the exact line, but the insight-extractor's `**Anchor**` field records only the path and whether it still resolves, and the PR's own worked citations are `path:line` coordinates (`AGENTS.md:252`, `python3-typing/SKILL.md:37`, `2026-03-10-cocoindex-code-improvements.md:37`). The fix is to make presence evidence re-locatable in the same way absence evidence already is. Note also that a bare `test -e` result proves only that a path resolved at that moment \u2014 it records nothing that re-locates the content the claim was actually about.\\n\\nBoth checks are local and deterministic: they need no network and return the same answer on every run, so a sandbox with no egress executes the gate in full.\\n\\nThis is the one behaviour salvaged from the two closing PRs that bites on *recurring* drift rather than a one-time cleanup, because it tests the live tree at write time rather than auditing a corpus afterwards \u2014 at write time the drift has not happened yet, so a path that resolves then was genuinely correct when cited.\\n\\nInteracts with #3534 (Gate 4 cannot express path drift): both concern when a claim is checked relative to when it is acted on. This item is the check before the irreversible action; #3534 is the audit that runs afterwards, once drift has had time to occur.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the original wording is in closed PR #3528, commits `fffe6a3df` (the filing gate) and `bf729ea90` (the Target-state exemption). Those commits also carry a `for p in ...; do test -e ...; done` loop and a flowchart gate node; the surrounding rubric rework they rode on was not salvaged, so treat the commits as source material rather than as a patch to apply, and note that the `test -e` loop satisfies the existence check but not the re-locatable-evidence requirement above. `.claude/agents/research-insight-extractor.md` was held by another agent during the salvage session and could not be edited there.\\n\",\"digest\":\"5648188d8ea1e889be5fdcc65482e46af0ffede8418825661f2a114c2b08a4ea\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABUPuxsw\"}"}